WEBVTT

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.460
I want to talk to you a little bit this morning about what we do in Crew Escape.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:24.950
Talk to you a little bit about our hardware, what each component does to help protect for the crew

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:27.370
an event of a number of emergencies that occur.

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:28.590
First thing, when the crew...

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:34.760
gets ready to don the suit we're going to talk about launch and then there's one little change

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:38.640
for entry there's a little difference when they get ready to come home but for launch first thing

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.080
they'll do is they'll put on some what we call lightweight underwear it's simply just a comfort

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:47.140
garment underneath keeps them nice and cool and it feels good on the skin this is what that is

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:55.560
this lightweight underwear then they put on what's called liquid cooling garment or lcg and the lcg

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:59.900
what it offers them is liquid cold liquid that runs across their body and across

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.620
their skin as they get ready for launch and as well as for entry is hooked up to a cooling system

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:11.090
that's hooked up to a cooling system that's attached to their seat. It's located on their

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:16.730
seat. It's a thermoelectric cooling system that allows them to get cool. They can actually control

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:21.470
the amount of cooling that they want for launch and entry. Some prefer cooling, some don't prefer

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:26.110
any cooling at all. So it's really a crew comfort item, but it does give them some cooling via the

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:29.920
liquid cooling garment. The G-Suit is worn during entry. It allows them to

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.480
they pressurize the bladder system.

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.340
My bladder system allows them to pressurize the G-suit

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:36.180
on the abdomen, lower extremities,

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.640
to keep that fluid up at the top of the body

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:39.820
while they're coming home.

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:42.890
As I said before, our suit is a full pressure suit.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:46.600
It's operating pressure is 3.5 PSIA.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:50.420
The rear entry suit, the first don the legs.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.500
Once they get the legs into the suit,

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.620
they'll go ahead and kind of get situated

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:56.400
like almost a turtle, if you will.

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:57.640
You're trying to get into that shell.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:01:59.840
they'll put their arms into the

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.990
into the arm sections. Then they'll punch through the helmet section or the head section

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:10.230
and through what we call the neck dam material. This is similar to like a wetsuit type material.

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:15.160
It allows them to maintain a different pressure in the helmet cavity than the lower torso.

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:20.220
We have two different pressure cavities in the suit itself. It's designed that way to help

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:24.580
give them oxygen when they're actually breathing a little bit different than like an EMU suit,

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:25.480
the white suit itself.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.610
The suit itself is constructed of only a few layers.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:38.070
The outside is a Nomex anti-abrasion layer, and that's the orange part here.

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:43.670
It's also flame retardant in the event that the crew has to be evac from smoke or from

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:47.310
a fire scenario, post-landing for example, so it's also designed to help protect them

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:50.950
for a very short period of time as they egress through a harmful situation like that.

00:02:51.250 --> 00:02:53.430
This is what we call a pass-through, a cooling pass-through.

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:57.830
They will connect this to the actual cooling unit itself, which will allow them to start

00:02:57.830 --> 00:02:59.970
getting the cold water into the suit itself. So it's...

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.120
actual penetration in the suit pressure bladder itself and that's what we'll do

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:07.780
and that's always on the right-hand side once they get into the suit the next

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:11.100
thing they'll do is just go ahead and don the gloves similar construction

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:15.230
no makes on the outside and there's a bladder on the inside as a male and a

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:18.730
female half they'd like they dock it there's an alignment fitting then they

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:22.150
rotate to lock it and there's a double locking feature so that there's it

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:25.520
prevents an inadvertent release of course which we don't want so there's a

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:28.800
couple of actions the crew would have to actually implement to release the

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:29.000
fitting.

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:29.680
But once they get.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.360
in the suit. They'll put on the infamous, what we call the Snoopy cap. It's basically the main

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:39.150
method that allows them to talk between crew members within the vehicle as well as to ground.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:45.870
Okay, the next item they'll put on, once they get the CCA on, this will carefully don the helmet,

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:52.190
the launch entry helmet, the LEH. It, of course, consists of a couple of things. It'll have the

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:57.170
neck ring, the helmet ring assembly, which will connect to the helmet ring on the suit side.

00:03:57.690 --> 00:03:59.970
It also consists of the pressure visor.

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:11.000
which is the main item that actually holds the pressure, also protects the face from any impacts of that nature.

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:14.940
This fitting here, the swivel fitting, is where we connect to the orbiter.

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:16.990
The orbiter supply is 100% O2.

00:04:17.230 --> 00:04:22.460
It diverts oxygen up to the helmet, which will come inside when the crew inhales.

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:29.800
It also will divert some oxygen into the G-suit through this penetration here, so the crew can dial in at their...

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.080
pleasure, how much pressure they actually want in the G-suit. Obviously that's entry

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:41.160
only. I'm going to go ahead and connect the helmet. For launch today, when we do it on the ground,

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:46.560
we have 40 minutes to do the entire event. So the seven crew members come in, we're given 40

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:51.380
minutes, start to stop. Very, very strict schedule, obviously, to get them out and they walk out on

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:56.300
time. So generally it takes us, if everything goes well, 20 to 30 minutes for all seven crew members.

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:59.960
The system is designed to, when they inhale, the helmet regulates.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.060
that's in the back here will actually sense the person is inhaling and it'll

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:09.720
open up and allow oxygen to come into the suit itself. When that occurs, that

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:13.920
oxygen flow, we have some other components in the suit in the helmet on

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:20.070
the left side here we call pressure switches that will detect that oxygen

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:24.070
is coming in and it'll actually cut the microphone. The parachute, the suit, the

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:27.960
helmet, the gloves, everything underneath is roughly about 91 pounds.

00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:29.790
And the helmet is a

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.500
call a non-conformal helmet, a dome helmet. Unlike your motorcycle helmet, which actually

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:40.520
holds your body and moves with you, we actually have a non-conformal helmet, so it's a bubble

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:45.300
helmet, so you can move your head relative to the helmet itself. So the helmet, most

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:48.250
of the weight rides on your shoulder and not on your head.
